The gap was READING, not detection. The cron full-suite self-claims gate went
red at ea22d7023c on 09-19 and sat unfiled for four days of sessions that merged
past it (INV-fugus), the third occurrence of that symptom. On 2026-09-23 it
happened again: full-suite went red at d31439e6, and it was noticed only because
a premise check happened to run `ci-debug cron-status`. LIVE.md's "run
cron-status at session start" was a memory aid, not a mechanism. The owner's
08-30 decision to keep detection on the cron cadence is not reopened.

`.agent/hooks/_shared/cron_status_nudge.py`, called from every mode branch of
session_start_logic.sh as `_append_cron_status` (all four vendors source it):
- Runs `scripts/ci-debug cron-status` (approved script; AGENTS.md allowed
  use-case 2) and parses each red workflow's failing STEPS.
- Walks back one cron verdict at a time (`cron-status <sha>^`) while the same
  step stays red, to find where it FIRST went red, which is where a bisect
  starts. The walk is bounded by a step count and a 25 s deadline. A failed
  lookup reads "not determined", never a guess.
- Suppresses a step when an OPEN row names it AND one of the red commits. The
  step alone would match any row that ever mentioned `test-all-packages`.
  Suppression is recomputed on every call, so a row filed in the meantime
  silences it at once.
- Is silent when green or pending, when the host is unreachable, when output
  can't be parsed, and under `CI` / `HG_SKIP_CRON_NUDGE`. Any exception is
  swallowed.
- Caches the network half in .git/cron-status-nudge.json for an hour.

